1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:12,390
A centrifuge is a machine that uses rapid rotation to apply centrifugal force to its

2
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:15,430
contents, separating them by density.

3
00:00:15,430 --> 00:00:19,130
The higher density materials will sink to the bottom of the container, while lower

4
00:00:19,130 --> 00:00:22,530
density materials will rise to the top.

5
00:00:22,530 --> 00:00:24,350
Centrifuges come in multiple sizes.

6
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:28,870
You can choose the size of your centrifuge based on the speed required, tube size, or

7
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:32,950
volume of the sample, and space available in your lab.

8
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:36,310
A few things to remember when loading your centrifuge.

9
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:43,390
Always wear the appropriate PPE. The minimum required PPE are lab coat, long pants,

10
00:00:43,390 --> 00:00:48,350
fully enclosed shoes, safety glasses, and gloves.

11
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:52,270
Balance the load by weighing each tube to ensure that they are evenly matched and

12
00:00:52,270 --> 00:00:55,070
distribute them directly across from each other.

13
00:00:55,070 --> 00:01:00,510
If you only have one sample, prepare a dummy tube to properly balance the load.

14
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:05,030
Confirm that the buckets and tubes are properly fitted, dry, and clean.

15
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:07,830
Then, properly secure the lid.

16
00:01:07,830 --> 00:01:11,950
Ensure the centrifuge is on a sturdy, level workspace before starting the

17
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:13,070
centrifuge.

18
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:17,950
If the centrifuge is wobbling or shaking, stop it immediately or pull the plug.

19
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:21,550
It is important to note that this will only stop power to the rotor, which will

20
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:25,510
still spin until it is slowed to a stop by friction.

21
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:29,830
Wait until the rotor has completely stopped before opening the lid.

22
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:34,230
A wobbling centrifuge usually occurs when the rotor is not balanced, and it may

23
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:40,310
cause significant damage and injure the operator and other laboratory personnel.

24
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:44,030
This concludes the segment on how to safely handle a centrifuge.

25
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:47,750
For more information about centrifuges, check out the centrifuge fact sheet

26
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:49,150
linked below.

27
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:58,150
If you have questions, please contact ISU-EHNS at 515-294-5359 or ehsinfo

28
00:01:58,150 --> 00:01:59,910
at iastate.edu.
